{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh: stop manually repeating the entire CI pipeline for small documentation changes.\n\nTwo completed Tailapp corrections provide a useful pilot. Request #4045 changed only two bundled READMEs; #4065 changed only the MCP reference page. Their primary artifacts record eleven successful local CI checks taking 105.40 and 102.31 seconds respectively, including both module test suites, race tests, vet, module checks, vulnerability scans and the demo. Both also received successful PR CI and main CI. The attached [validation evidence](validation-evidence.json) preserves the exact heads, changed paths, local check records and GitHub job timings. These are reported local timings, not my reruns; I am not adding overlapping runs into a claimed delivery-time saving.\n\n**Recommendation:** give each check a clear job. Before review, the author runs the checks needed to establish the changed behavior and submits the candidate to normal CI. For these documentation changes that means checking the described behavior against its source, relative links, file previews and the absence of unrelated changes. The reviewer independently checks those claims and reads the relevant CI results. Repeating the full CI pipeline locally should answer a stated question—such as a platform difference, an unexplained failure or evidence that cannot be verified.\n\nKeep all required CI gates, including main integration checks. Record the source head, actual tested checkout/tree, environment, commands and result link; a PR label or green badge alone is insufficient. A synthetic PR merge and its candidate are distinct. New source, dependency, workflow or relevant environment changes need new evidence. Platform-specific behavior still needs that platform's proof. Documentation embedded in binaries, executable examples and generated assets need their corresponding checks; a `.md` extension is not enough to choose validation.\n\nThis preserves the [existing exact-head and independent-review rules](AGENTS.md@07753351d69c05dedec58f4e2561a793054312a5:58). It changes neither approval authority nor merge admission. It also needs no new task state, evidence cache or test-selection framework.\n\nPilot this on the next three ordinary documentation repairs: put a short “local checks / CI evidence / independent checks” paragraph in the primary report, link results from companion artifacts, and record duplicate runs and review turnaround. Compare with these two cases before changing CI itself. Keep existing request-specific validation promises unless their author changes them.\n\nThis is a recommendation for your review, not an adopted policy or an instruction to weaken current checks.\n","body":{"head":"07753351d69c05dedec58f4e2561a793054312a5","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a1c15fb28f5df85b922f551195b7b736f2488138 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:04075a23bf804a9e61f04055021af9a18d6ee9ca, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0739dc3d9833620e90976f7858fbc66a9a781d8d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:07e8e48166ea90a1d864c829f36902817926f6e7 and 39 more"}}